TODO: pick a licence before the first public release.

The brief called for MIT or AGPL and left the choice open. The tradeoff:

  MIT   — maximum adoption. Anyone can ship a closed-source e-ink reader built
          on this core, including a hardware vendor who never contributes back.

  AGPL  — improvements to a distributed derivative must be published. For a
          device that runs entirely on-device with no network service, the
          "network use" clause that makes AGPL distinctive rarely triggers, so
          in practice it behaves close to GPLv3 here.

Whichever is chosen, replace this file with its full text and add the matching
SPDX identifier to the source headers.

Third-party material bundled in this repository keeps its own terms and is not
affected by this choice:

  assets/fonts/       Literata — SIL Open Font License 1.1 (see OFL.txt)
  third_party/stb/    stb_truetype.h, stb_image_write.h — public domain
  third_party/doctest doctest.h — MIT
